<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques Callot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1592–<a href="/years/1635">1635</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques Callot was a printmaker and draughtsman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> for the Medici before returning home around 1621. He produced more than 1,400 etchings and greatly refined the technique. His series <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-miseries-and-misfortunes-of-war">The Miseries and Misfortunes of War</a>, published in 1633, depicts the brutality of soldiers during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-callot.jpg" alt="Jacques Callot" width="239" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Vorsterman (1595-1675) after Anthony van Dyck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacobus_Callot_after_A._van_Dyck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Callot was born and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a>, the capital of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, then an independent state on the north-eastern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He came from an important family; his father was master of ceremonies at the ducal court, and he often described himself as noble in the inscriptions to his prints. At fifteen he was apprenticed to a goldsmith but soon travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he learned engraving from the expatriate Frenchman Philippe Thomassin. He probably then studied etching with Antonio Tempesta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, where he lived from 1612 to 1621. More than 2,000 preparatory drawings survive, but no paintings by him are known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Florence Callot became an independent master and often worked for the Medici court. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-ii-de-medici">Cosimo II de' Medici</a> in 1621 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a>, where he lived for the rest of his life, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> later in the decade. The courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> commissioned work from him, as did publishers, mostly in Paris. His prints were distributed widely through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rembrandt">Rembrandt</a> was a keen collector. He made more than 1,400 etchings of soldiers, clowns, drunkards, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, beggars and court life, as well as religious and military subjects, and most were small, at most about 15 centimetres on their longest dimension.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Callot developed the échoppe, an etching needle with a slanting oval end that let etchers make a swelling line like an engraver's. He also seems to have introduced an etching ground of lute-makers' varnish in place of a wax-based one, so that lines could be etched more deeply and the risk of foul-biting was reduced, allowing detailed work that had been the monopoly of engravers. He used multiple stoppings-out more extensively than earlier etchers, achieving subtle effects of distance and light and shade. Abraham Bosse spread his innovations with the first published manual of etching.</p><p>His best known prints are two series on the Miseries and Misfortunes of War: 18 prints published in 1633 and the earlier, incomplete Les Petites Misères. They show soldiers pillaging and burning through towns, country and convents, before being arrested and executed by their superiors, lynched by peasants or surviving as crippled beggars. In 1633 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> was invaded by the French during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. The series is still ranked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-goya">Francisco Goya</a>'s Disasters of War, which it influenced, among the most powerful artistic statements of the inhumanity of war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacques Callot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The printmaker <b>Jacques Callot</b> of Nancy recorded the horrors of the Thirty Years’ War in The Miseries and Misfortunes of War (1633), Georges de La Tour worked at Lunéville, and the landscape painter Claude Lorrain, born at Chamagne, took his name from his homeland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nancy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Nancy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.69,6.18&amp;year=1619" aria-label="Nancy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4693 463 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jacques Callot"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5143 733h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5143 733h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.69,6.18&amp;year=1619">What was here in 1619? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
